SM(274,{"n":"Genesis 48","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 48.mp3","d":2951.5,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.04,"Okay. And when we get started, we'll be picking up in uh Genesis chapter 48, verse 1."],[39.44,"We may well finish Genesis this evening, but who knows?"],[44.56,"Uh when I make such predictions, they're usually wrong."],[48.48,"So we don't have to do too much review tonight because this last part of Genesis, in particular, chapters 48 and 49 are sort of its own textual unit."],[64.52,"I'll talk about that more here in a minute."],[67.48,"So we really, last time we really finished up the story of Joseph, which was really sort of an extension of the story of Jacob, as we talked about, because Jacob is still around."],[85.56,"And at the end of that story, Jacob ended up traveling himself to Egypt, being reunited with Joseph."],[95.64,"We saw the brothers are also there, sort of the whole extended family and household of Jacob is now living in the Nile Delta region."],[105.2,"And we also saw this sort of the beginning of this transformation in Egyptian life that started happening during this period."],[116.88,"There was this crisis of the seven years of famine."],[120.56,"Joseph prepared for it, but in the process of sort of surviving that and the measures that Joseph took with Pharaoh, there was sort of a centralization of authority."],[131.12,"There were certain economic shifts that we saw talked about."],[135.6,"And those are all going to play into, as we mentioned last time, when we come into Exodus."],[142.24,"Remember, Genesis, we've said this all along."],[144.4,"Genesis is the prologue to the Torah."],[147.6,"So this is the prologue. to what's going to happen in Exodus through Deuteronomy"],[153.56,"And Exodus through Deuteronomy is going to be the arc of the life of Moses, essentially."],[158.76,"Deuteronomy ends with his death, Exodus begins with his birth."],[163.32,"But that is some centuries after Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob and his sons."],[169.4,"And so we're going to arrive at this point at the beginning of Exodus, centuries later, where there's going to be a very different relationship between Pharaoh, the then current Pharaoh from this dynasty, and the Israelite people."],[185.12,"And we saw last time the beginnings of that transformation that they had it had its roots in this crisis."],[193.48,"And this is just an obser.vable"],[196.08,"This is one of many places where we see this sort of real historical verisimilitude in the historical narratives of the Old Testament, in the sense that it does tend to be in times of crisis when there are major changes to the order within nations."],[213.02,"I mean, this is just a truth throughout history."],[217.28,"And this is one example."],[219.2,"And it's just the beginning, but we're going to see the fruit of it when we start uh Exodus."],[224.64,"Um, so that sort of narrative has wrapped up."],[227.92,"So what we're gonna be reading tonight, particularly chapters 48 and 49, are what is called the testament of Jacob."],[238.16,"And this is testament in the sense in which we talk about someone's last will and testament."],[238.48,"this is Where going to be sort of Jacob's parting words to his sons, to some of his son's sons."],[255.41,"To members of his extended family before his death."],[278.67,"In the sense we usually use prophetic."],[281.09,"There's a fuller orb sense."],[282.57,"We'll talk about that more when we talk about Moses being a prophet."],[285.77,"But the way we commonly use it is predicting the future."],[288.67,"Saying something about the character of the descendants of these people, the tribes that are going to be named after these people."],[296.45,"This is yet another way in which this is serving as a prologue to what's going to happen in the rest of the Torah."],[303.49,"We need to keep that in mind that this is talking about people groups."],[308.78,"Later people groups."],[309.98,"Because, like, there's some of these that are gonna sound really harsh, you know, and it's like, wow, was Jacob on his deathbed just really ticked off at one of his kids?"],[321.5,"Like, can you imagine if those were your dad's last word to you?"],[325.44,"It's not dire.cted at right, this individual, but it's directed at the tribe that's going to bear their name later"],[332.76,"This people group."],[334.81,"Which we'll be talking about this more in detail when we get into Exodus."],[340.09,"Um, but it bears saying here, we also have to get uh the idea of genetics out of our brain, or at least suspend it for a moment."],[355.21,"Because it didn't exist right at the time this is written."],[359.55,"So we're gonna see, it's gonna be very plain in Exodus."],[363.47,"We're gonna see that, for example, the people in the tribe of Dan are not all or even mostly going to be biological descendants of the person Dan, the son of Jacob."],[381.91,"It's gonna be very clear."],[383.43,"This isn't like revisionist."],[385.19,"This is like you read the text closely."],[387.64,"The Levites are not mostly going to be descended from the person Levi."],[394.68,"That's gonna be very clear in the text that this is true."],[397.96,"So people groups, even though they bear the names of these people and they look to these people as their father, that's more in the sense that we would use the term founding father, like in the United States, than in the sense of, oh, we are all literally biologically related to this person."],[419.13,"So most people who would say that George Washington is the father of our country are not his biological descendants."],[426.62,"So fatherhood is doing different work there, than just biological work."],[434.0,"What makes people groups in the ancient Near East, is not like phenotype, not what you look like."],[444.3,"Because let's be honest, most people in the ancient Near East looked roughly identical."],[451.68,"When you start getting into Africa, you start getting into even darker skin tones, but we're talking we're not talking about people who had radical differences in physiognomy."],[461.0,"There weren't any pale, blue-eyed, blonde-haired people running around in the ancient Near East."],[466.85,"Like so it wasn't about like race the way we think about it in modern times."],[476.26,"And there was no DNA testing, there was no way to prove who you were biologically descended from."],[483.72,"Or demonstrate that."],[485.62,"So people groups were made up of cultural and societal bonds, largely based in certain rituals."],[497.24,"Coming of age rituals, rituals. at the birth of a child"],[500.88,"Ritual. And this is a super important concept to even understand what the rest of the Torah is doing."],[510.53,"Because right now there is not a nation Israel."],[514.53,"There's a family."],[516.93,"They're actually Mesopotamians, technically."],[521.47,"Because they're mostly biological, and even then we're including servants and stuff, but mostly biologically descended from Abraham, even only mostly at this point."],[531.6,"And there's going to be a nation of Israel."],[534.56,"And so a lot of what the rest of the Torah is going to be doing is going to be defining what that means."],[538.88,"So you're going to have rituals like circumcision."],[542.66,"With the Zabirth ritual."],[543.7,"You're going to have coming of age rituals."],[545.22,"You're going to have ritual worship and patterns of worship."],[548.02,"You're going to have worship directed to a particular God."],[551.43,"And ways in which that is done."],[553.11,"And feasts and festivals that are celebrated together as a community."],[556.76,"These are the things that are going to identify a people group."],[560.52,"And so someone can be adopted into a people group."],[566.76,"And once they've been adopted into a people group, then they are a part of that people group just as much as someone who was born into it."],[577.15,"And so we're going to see, for example, just to give one example of this that's very clear in the Bible. but we're first introduced to the person Caleb, of Joshua and Caleb"],[588.55,"We're told he's a Keneshite, meaning not an Israelite, a Canaanite."],[595.49,"He's literally a Canaanite."],[598.37,"At the end of the book of Joshua, where we end up with Caleb, he's one of the elders of the tribe of Judah."],[606.45,"Not just a member of the tribe of Judah, one of the elders of the tribe of Judah."],[611.09,"So he and all of his descendants would say, we're Judahites, we're of Judah."],[615.81,"Judah is our father."],[621.19,"Even though that's not true from our like biological perspective."],[625.19,"Is there some kind of shift that happens in the New? Testament"],[628.51,"Because there's the whole interaction where it's like we're the children of Abraham, and it's like we can raise them up from the that shift happens in the latter part of the Old Testament."],[642.03,"It happened Ezra and Nehemiah after the return from exile."],[645.31,"That's where you get the prohibitions on intermarriage."],[648.59,"Okay, okay. Across the board."],[650.54,"We talked about last time with Joseph and Aseneth."],[653.2,"He marries an Egyptian, and there doesn't seem to be any issue about it."],[656.8,"Whereas they're told not to marry those certain Canaanite groups."],[660.5,"But there's not a general prohibition on."],[663.36,"That prohibition comes after the exile."],[665.8,"Okay So that's where that shift starts happening."],[669.69,"Um And they start to become there starts to be an you could talk about an ethnic Judean."],[676.12,"But even then, it's not impermeable."],[679.43,"Um, and e like after the exile, we'll see that there's a there's a tribe of people in the land who help the returning people of Judah, sort of ally with them, and basically get told, okay, you're now members of the tribe of Issachar."],[697.67,"And they're like, oh, okay, cool."],[699.36,"Like, but like, genetically they're not."],[702.88,"But then now they're Israelites, so now you could intermarry with them."],[706.82,"And and you get you get crazier stuff than that, but we'll get into this more."],[712.54,"Probably when we get into the beginning of Deuteronomy, you get in in 1 Maccabees, there's the text of the treaty between uh newly independent Judea or the Hasmoneans and Sparta, where they say the Spartans are their fellow children of Abraham."],[732.22,"Their fellow descendants of Abraham."],[734.14,"It's like, what?"],[736.32,"Like, but we'll get into that a little more."],[738.24,"But right, so there's even then, even after that shift happens, there's still a lot more permeability than we normally think."],[748.26,"But a lot of us have had drummed in from a frankly bad reading of the New Testament, have had drummed into our head that salvation or the people of God or something is an ethnic thing in the Old Testament and then becomes open to everybody in the New Testament, and that's simply not true."],[770.22,"Simply not true."],[773.42,"So ancient Israel, especially in this earliest phase, is just as ethnically diverse as the church."],[782.56,"Now, at different points in history, that'll be in Old Testament history, that'll be true to varying degrees."],[790.17,"But at least at the start."],[792.57,"And we're gonna see crazy stuff."],[794.89,"Crazy stuff. Like the third high priest of Israel is gonna be a black African."],[799.97,"People are gonna say, What?"],[801.81,"If you put that in a movie today, everyone would say it was woke."],[805.33,"And it wouldn't be, it would be historically accurate."],[807.81,"But anyway. So yeah, so in this testament, when he's talking to Dan, or he's talking to Judah, or he's talking to Joseph or Jude uh Jacob."],[819.99,"Jacob is really speaking concerning those peoples."],[825.13,"We're sort of getting the twelve tribes introduced to us here at the end of Genesis for when we encounter them now in Exodus."],[835.81,"right Through these prophetic words from Jacob."],[839.95,"And this format of a a testament is going to become staple of Jewish literature."],[848.87,"So this pattern is going to be followed."],[851.91,"There's tons of Jewish literature, most of it from the probably the 4th century BC to the 1st century AD, that are in this testament format."],[866.95,"So there's a testament of Adam that's supposed to be his last words to Seth and Cain."],[872.31,"He actually says some negative stuff to Cain, obviously."],[875.29,"And positive stuff to Seth."],[876.81,"But uh, there's a testament of Moses."],[879.53,"There's a there's Testament of the 12 patriarchs that are each of the 12 sons of Jacob giving their parting words."],[886.97,"Like to their descendants"],[889.22,"The Testament of the Twelve Patriarchs is actually a really important apocryphal text."],[898.02,"And as an aside, the Testament of the Twelve Patriarchs, we have copies from two places: the Dead Sea Scrolls, where they were buried in a Jewish community in the first century AD, and from Mount Athos, where it was copied for 15 centuries by monks."],[915.48,"But so the testament, this becomes there's all kinds of other figures too."],[918.44,"There's a testament of Enoch, there's a testament of Abraham."],[921.96,"They proliferate. Uings this format because it's a way they use this format to speak prophetically."],[930.19,"To use the vehicle of the parting words of one of these major biblical figures to talk about the history up to their day of Israel and to cast it in a certain way, and to cast their own generation in a certain way."],[948.19,"But so this originates with these couple of chapters."],[950.75,"So these are an important couple of chapters in terms of Torah interpretation, within Jewish communities in the Old Testament period in particular."],[964.37,"I don't think, interestingly enough, someone can correct me."],[970.53,"But I I have seen no evidence that they hold the. same kind of importance and influence in rabbinic Judaism"],[978.41,"They're seen, I think, now as mostly being just related to the rest of the Hebrew Bible."],[986.49,"Like, oh yeah, this prophesies things that happened in the rest of the Hebrew Bible, and that's sort of the end of it."],[992.01,"And they're not that important."],[993.29,"But they were critically important, these chapters, for example, in the writing of the New Testament."],[1000.17,"And we'll see some of that as we go through tonight."],[1003.69,"So, with all that, that wasn't really recap, that was more intro."],[1009.69,"But uh, with all that, unless there's any more comments, questions, we will go ahead and pick up in Genesis chapter. 48, verse 1"],[1021.97,"Now it came to pass after these things that Joseph was told, indeed, your father is sick, and he took with him his two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim, and went to Jacob."],[1036.53,"Okay, so these are his two sons."],[1038.29,"Manasseh is the older one of the two."],[1042.69,"Note Joseph is gonna go with his two sons to his father and get this prophecy before his brothers."],[1056.17,"So this is related to that he's the firstborn of Rachel, therefore, sort of the one favored by Jacob."],[1068.87,"And gets this pronounced the place."],[1071.43,"Now his two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim, are sometimes going to be referred to in English translations of the Old Testament as half tribes."],[1082.63,"Like the half tribe of Manasseh, the half tribe of Ephraim."],[1086.55,"And that's because they're both descended from Joseph."],[1090.73,"Joseph's brothers, the other tribes take their names from Joseph's brothers, but there's not a tribe, Joseph."],[1099.07,"There's two tribes, Manasseh and Ephraim, that both have Joseph as their father."],[1105.55,"And so the way it's talked about, technically, there will be sort of 13 tribes."],[1112.6,"Even though there's 12 tribes."],[1114.04,"This won't be the only time we'll see that."],[1115.88,"When we get to the Ten Commandments, we'll see that there's actually like eleven."],[1119.48,"And that's why everyone numbers them differently, because that's which ones you stick together."],[1123.48,"Because there's really like 11."],[1124.92,"But the same way here, there's actually sort of 13 tribes, but two of them see."],[1130.57,"Joseph as their father."],[1131.93,"We mentioned this a little in the book of Revelation, because remember, we got to the list of the 12 tribes, and Dan had been left out."],[1138.35,"And Manasseh and Ephraim were listed separately."],[1140.75,"So there were still 12 with Dan missing."],[1143.6,"Would that be 10 plus Manasseh now?"],[1147.6,"That were listed in Revelation, yeah."],[1149.68,"Yeah. Two of the original tribes."],[1150.64,"And so then Manasseh and Ephraim were listed separately"],[1151.88,"One of Dan got dropped out."],[1157.16,"So if you listed Dan in, you'd have 13."],[1160.52,"You'd have 13."],[1161.32,"Yeah. If you added Dan in, you'd have 13."],[1164.52,"There's 12 plus two halves."],[1166.74,"No, there's 11 plus two halves."],[1169.06,"Oh, okay. Okay."],[1170.26,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[1172.02,"So there's gonna be two tribes that have these two names."],[1177.14,"Verse 2, thus Jacob was told, look, your son Joseph is coming to you."],[1181.46,"And Israel strengthened himself and sat up on the bed."],[1185.38,"Then Jacob said to Joseph, My God appeared to me at Luz in the land of Canaan and blessed me, and said to me, Behold, I will increase and multiply you, and I will make of you a gathering of nations, and give this land to your seed after you as an everlasting possession."],[1206.22,"Notice appeared to me."],[1210.32,"Emphasizing that. And that's something that's yeah, that hasn't happened with Joseph."],[1216.73,"So we've talked about how there's this transition happening."],[1219.93,"Verse 5 Now therefore your two sons, Ephraim and Manasseh, who are born to you in the land of Egypt, before I came to you in Egypt, are mine, as Reuben and Simeon, they shall be mine."],[1234.29,"Isn't that a shik and Ruby Reuben?"],[1237.25,"Yes, it is."],[1239.03,"And why are there issues with Reuben and Simeon?"],[1241.67,"Because they murdered all of them."],[1242.79,"They led the massacre of Shechem."],[1246.76,"But he's basically saying, this is a little more of the favoritism."],[1252.12,"I'm gonna treat your two sons as if they were my sons."],[1255.72,"Well, what does that mean?"],[1257.64,"Well, that's talking about shares of inheritance and that kind of thing."],[1262.97,"Notice here also, remember there was this whole thing about who was going to receive the promises that had come from Abraham to Isaac."],[1272.17,"Was it gonna be Jacob or Esau?"],[1274.35,"What is Jacob doing here in his mind?"],[1278.11,"Giving them to Joseph."],[1279.07,"Joseph. In terms of's sons"],[1285.05,"Even though Joseph, not his firstborn son by a long shot."],[1290.55,"So yeah, this is what he's intentionally doing."],[1292.63,"But notice something's already been telegraphed here."],[1294.95,"It was telegraphed kind of subtly."],[1296.79,"It's going to be unsubtle in a minute."],[1298.63,"Verse 5, notice your two sons, Ephraim and Manasseh."],[1302.79,"He just named them in reverse order."],[1306.45,"Verse 6, your offspring whom you beget after them shall take possession of their inheritances within the tribes of their brothers."],[1316.99,"So he's saying, any more kids you have will take their inheritance within Manasseh and Ephraim."],[1326.44,"That's why we don't have three third tribes or four quarter tribes."],[1331.02,"We have two half tribes."],[1333.9,"Father, by reversing the order with Ephraim and Manasseh, it's almost kind of like saying that's why I put you, Joseph, in front of your oldest brother."],[1343.42,"Well, we'll see."],[1343.98,"There's gonna be more here with that."],[1345.82,"Yeah. Verse 7, but as for me, when I came from Mesopotamia of Syria, Rachel died beside me in the land of Canaan on the way, and there was but a little distance to go to Ephraith, and I buried her there on the way to Ephraith."],[1360.86,"That is Bethlehem."],[1363.1,"That is Bethlehem, very clearly added later."],[1366.94,"To tell you where Ephraith is"],[1371.88,"Now there are still places, we just read one since we're just coming off of Christmas, where Bethlehem is referred to as Ephratha later on to call us back to this."],[1383.58,"This is the first appearance of that city."],[1385.82,"That city under the name Bethlehem is going to become important when David is born there."],[1392.22,"Much later, like centuries after this."],[1395.71,"Many centuries after this."],[1397.87,"Almost a millennium after this."],[1400.43,"But this is sort of its first appearance."],[1405.31,"Then Israel saw Joseph's sons and said, Who are these with you?"],[1409.23,"He doesn't literally mean like, what"],[1412.79,"I've never seen right, but he's, you know, asking them to be presented to him."],[1418.19,"So Joseph said to his father, They are my sons, God gave me here."],[1422.27,"Jacob then said, Bring them to me that I may bless them."],[1427.63,"Now the eyes of Israel were dim by reason of old age, and he could not see."],[1432.27,"Then Joseph brought them near him and he kissed and embraced them."],[1435.63,"So that's how familiar the scene sounded familiar at all to you."],[1438.75,"Remember, Isaac was blind, and so."],[1441.73,"He came in."],[1442.45,"So this is very clearly he's intending to pass the promises and the inheritance on to them."],[1449.65,"Verse 11: Then Israel said to Joseph, I had not thought to see your face, but in fact God has shown me your seed."],[1454.05,"Now not only do I see your face I see your sons, my grandsons."],[1456.01,"So he said, because he thought he was dead."],[1457.99,"He's saying, I thought I'd never see your face again."],[1465.29,"So Joseph brought them from beside his knees, and they bowed down with their face to the ground."],[1470.65,"Then Joseph took them both, Ephraim with his right hand toward Israel's left hand, and Manasseh with his left hand toward Israel's right hand, and brought them near him."],[1482.33,"So what is this getting at?"],[1483.85,"So he's gonna bless them, with his hands."],[1486.35,"So he brings the older son to his right hand."],[1489.84,"The firstborn son to his right hand."],[1492.28,"Secondborn son to his left hand."],[1494.54,"Which would be the normal way that would happen if he's gonna bless them both."],[1497.34,"Because of course, the inheritance goes to the through the firstborn line"],[1507.46,"Thus, Israel stretched out his right hand and laid it on Ephraim's head, who was the younger, and his left hand on Manasseh's head, guiding his hands knowingly for Manasseh was the firstborn."],[1520.82,"So he crosses his arms and deliberately puts his right hand on the younger one's head."],[1530.36,"So he's making the younger one, giving the firstborn blessing to the younger one."],[1538.04,"Which is very both what happened with him him, because he was technically the younger one of and Esau."],[1541.48,"But it makes the point that he's doing this"],[1547.82,"It's not because he's blind, it's not because he's confused."],[1550.14,"He's doing this on purpose."],[1552.12,"So Ephraim is actually receiving the blessing of uh the fathers."],[1559.24,"Is this in any way related to when you put the L and something you cross?"],[1566.12,"No. No. But we'll get to that."],[1568.84,"Yeah. I'll think more about that."],[1574.28,"My first instinct is no, but I'll think more about that."],[1578.52,"So verse 15."],[1581.0,"Then he blessed them and said, God, before whom my fathers Abraham and Isaac were well pleasing, the God who has fed me all my life long to this day, the angel who redeemed me from all evil, bless these lads."],[1600.08,"Great translation. These lads."],[1605.04,"Let my name be named upon them, and the name of my fathers Abraham and Isaac, and let them grow into a great multitude in the midst of the earth."],[1616.8,"Notice something interesting here."],[1620.48,"We've got these three things named in parallel: the God of Abraham and Isaac, the God of Jacob, the angel."],[1630.96,"The one who he wrestled with"],[1634.36,"Best angel he wrestled with."],[1638.52,"The one who we saw, the ziggurat."],[1640.61,"The ladder. The one before the stone."],[1645.5,"The one who appeared to him."],[1647.68,"Remember, we've been talking about already in Genesis."],[1652.24,"This is setting us up for something that's going to be in the rest of the Torah."],[1655.92,"There is this second figure."],[1659.44,"This is the first time."],[1660.72,"Well, not the first time."],[1664.72,"One of the first times he's referred to by the title Angel or Messenger."],[1670.94,"But here we see, that there's this figure who is both differentiated from God and equated with God."],[1686.68,"There is this second figure."],[1690.04,"Well, some of the church fathers uh would say that the angel that Jacob saw on the ladder, that that was Christ."],[1698.46,"That's where we're going with this."],[1699.5,"Yeah. Yeah, you haven't been here for a lot of the past ones."],[1702.62,"Yeah. Yeah. But Jacob's saying this reflectively."],[1708.52,"This is something I've been pointing out as we go through."],[1713.21,"But this shows by Jacob speaking this way that Jacob understood this."],[1714.65,"Jacob's This is within understanding."],[1721.62,"That he understands that in a sense he has not seen God."],[1727.86,"But he also understands that he's seen God."],[1731.78,"And that the God who he has seen is not a different God from the one he hasn't seen."],[1738.27,"So this is all within his understanding of who God is."],[1743.63,"That's almost how inspiration seems to work as well."],[1746.75,"Like they're not, you know, cognizant or conscious that they're writing divine revelation."],[1753.31,"But yet it's almost like being revealed to them actually writing it."],[1756.99,"Yeah. But so this is important because."],[1762.76,"The idea of the Holy Trinity, particularly the idea of Christ being God, being divine, particular, is not this new thing that Christians come up with in the fourth century and then try to read back into the Bible."],[1781.0,"This idea exists within Judaism."],[1784.86,"Very clearly exists within Judaism for five centuries before Christ."],[1789.74,"But is even, I mean, it's here in the Torah."],[1793.76,"This understanding of God."],[1794.8,"And this is one of the passages that very clearly reflects it as being within their thought."],[1799.44,"And who's at least at the Ur level, I'm not going to go through all this again about the Mosaic origin of the Pentateuch, but Moses is here, Moses' own understanding that we're going to see in the life of Moses as we go forward to the Torah, is at the root of what's written here."],[1818.82,"Meaning these ideas are not only being presented as being within Jacob's understanding, but being within Moses' understanding."],[1826.63,"Of who God is."],[1829.49,"They do not think there's only one God who's only one person who no one has ever seen."],[1834.93,"Clearly, because they're recording having seen him."],[1838.43,"And eaten with him and done these other things with him."],[1841.71,"But so we can see how here Jacob can talk in this way."],[1846.23,"And this isn't confusing or weird or a logical contradiction, or right?"],[1850.63,"This is within his experience of God in his life."],[1856.31,"And his own understanding of it."],[1861.91,"Now when Joseph saw his father put his right hand on the head of Ephraim, it displeased him."],[1868.07,"So he took hold of his father's hand to remove it from Ephraim's head to Manasseh's head."],[1872.47,"So he tries to fix."],[1874.97,"Wait, wait, wait. right"],[1876.17,"Here's something ironic."],[1878.86,"Joseph is like, oh no, the firstborn son is supposed to get the self-awareness not detected."],[1887.67,"As the eleventh born son."],[1894.13,"So Joseph said to his father, Not so, my father, for this one is the firstborn, put your right hand on his head."],[1900.53,"But his father refused it, said, I know my son, I know."],[1904.45,"He also shall become a people, and he also shall be exalted, but truly his younger brother shall be greater than he, and his seed shall become a multitude of nations."],[1919.01,"So, because it's not immediately obvious in this translation, but there's something very important that just happened here."],[1927.81,"We've seen so far in this narrative, that the promises to Abraham are to his seed both in a singular and a plural sense."],[1941.9,"The Hebrew word Zarah is a collective noun."],[1944.7,"It's like fish or sheep in English."],[1948.72,"It could be singular or plural, depending on context."],[1953.17,"And there's a deliberate ambiguity here in Genesis, because it's both."],[1957.57,"So there's a sense in which the promises are to Abraham's descendants."],[1962.62,"The children of Abraham."],[1964.5,"Plural, broadly. But there's also a sense, as we've talked about, in which they're singular, in which there's this line."],[1972.2,"So it's Isaac, not Ishmael."],[1974.52,"Ishmael receives. an inheritance too, but the line is Isaac"],[1980.06,"And then from Isaac to Jacob, Esau receives an inheritance too, but right, the line goes through Jacob."],[1989.74,"What we just read, if we follow that through, would be the other brothers receive an inheritance too, but the line goes through Joseph."],[2002.92,"And as Jacob just literally said, Manasseh receives an inheritance too, but the line goes through Ephraim."],[2013.64,"So anybody who's read the rest of the Old Testament see a problem with that"],[2019.12,"Right? That's not how it ends up working out."],[2024.64,"But Ephraim is like the head of the world."],[2027.28,"Ephraim is the most numerous tribe, and the tribe that is the center of the northern kingdom of Israel."],[2037.3,"As opposed to Judah when they split."],[2043.22,"So you've got barely two kings while they're together."],[2047.7,"They're together for like an eye blink."],[2049.78,"They're together for like less than a century."],[2052.9,"We have to keep this timetable in mind."],[2054.98,"We tend to have this inflated timetable of how long there was an Israel in the world."],[2059.62,"So less than a century, there's a United Kingdom that exists."],[2064.7,"And that's the 10th century BC."],[2068.9,"Then you get less than 200 years of the Northern Kingdom of Israel existing, and then it's gone."],[2078.52,"So I'm not going to go into any further political comments on this, but uh before 1948, there was a nation of Israel in the world for less than 300 years in all of human history."],[2093.24,"Okay. So it's less than 200 years after that for the Northern Kingdom."],[2102.33,"And it's barely 300 for Judah."],[2108.29,"Nations are not around very long."],[2112.21,"They are not in terms of world history."],[2115.33,"They are not around that long."],[2118.05,"Just pretty much any country in Europe has been around longer than that."],[2121.81,"By multiples. There has been a France far longer than any of these existed."],[2127.99,"There has been an England far longer than I mean the United States is rivaling the Northern Kingdom of Israel already."],[2136.63,"So this seems weird from our perspective now of history."],[2143.0,"So let me say a couple things about this."],[2146.36,"First thing is this is one of the attestations to the antiquity of the Torah."],[2156.44,"So if we accept what our 19th century German friends, in critical biblical scholarship want to say, that the Torah was all written post exile."],[2169.82,"That's where they're really at now."],[2172.46,"Our actual 19th century German friends were a little more conservative than that."],[2176.22,"They said parts of it started being written in the 10th century BC."],[2181.02,"Bits and pieces."],[2183.0,"But now it's it's all post-exile."],[2185.66,"So it's all post-exile where those 10 tribes have ceased to exist."],[2188.86,"ceased to exist. The Northern Kingdom has"],[2191.52,"Where Ephraim has ceased to exist."],[2195.76,"And the capital city of Ephraim, Samaria, has become the home of the Samaritans who are hated and despised."],[2205.92,"Why would there be Ephraimite traditions like this in the Torah?"],[2210.4,"If it was written at that time."],[2220.64,"They would not be included."],[2223.56,"We'd have a pretty clear pro-Judah message here."],[2230.89,"They'd be the hero."],[2233.15,"And in fact, when you look at even very early books after the Torah Joshua judges."],[2241.83,"As we'll see when we go through Joshua Judges, especially judges."],[2246.79,"They are so pro-Judah."],[2249.59,"And Judahite monarchy in particular."],[2253.29,"They clearly present Judah as being the faithful tribe."],[2257.91,"So you could make a case for them, being written, especially judges, being written, post-David, at least."],[2270.29,"About the history leading up to that."],[2273.22,"So they're all colored by that later history."],[2275.06,"The Torah isn't"],[2280.91,"Right? The Torah includes the traditions of these tribes that cease to exist."],[2284.99,"The founders of these tribes that cease to exist."],[2290.03,"Why? So, first of all, this is, I think, incredibly compelling evidence that this has to date."],[2298.63,"At least the main body of what's contained in the Torah has to predate the actual establishment of the nature of Israel."],[2308.55,"At least the dividing of the kingdom."],[2312.6,"Has to have been set in some way in terms of the contents."],[2316.48,"Obviously, there are things added, like adding which is Bethlehem."],[2317.68,"After Ephraith But you get my point."],[2324.48,"Well, it would also have to be like weight too, so it would have to be older."],[2328.64,"Like it would have to be older in the sense of like if we're gonna copy over and over, we would have had to carry some kind of weight of history."],[2334.48,"Right to keep it the same."],[2336.48,"And we've seen smaller examples of this."],[2338.16,"Like I mentioned how there are the altars that get set up over the course of these narratives that end up being in the territory of the 12 different tribes."],[2346.08,"So it's very clear that in Genesis, especially, we've got traditions from these different tribes that are all being brought together here in one place."],[2359.86,"And again, if they've ceased to exist."],[2365.08,"And if Samaria now is despised."],[2367.96,"And by the way, while the two kingdoms were there next to each other, uh the northern kingdom hated Judah and kept trying to destroy it."],[2377.88,"We'll see that when we get into the later historical books."],[2380.76,"I don't think most people realize this."],[2383.86,"These things that go down, like the Syro Ephraimite War, where the northern the Ephraimites, the Northern Kingdom, team up with the Syrians to attack and try to conquer Judah."],[2397.39,"Or when Athaliah, Ahab's daughter, marries into the royal family of Judah and tries to exterminate the line of David."],[2403.63,"the And in response, Judahites help exterminate the line of Ahab."],[2413.29,"So they were not friends."],[2415.19,"Like, no one in Judah was interested in preserving the traditions of these dead tribes."],[2421.03,"They're interested in explaining they're all dead because they were wicked and evil."],[2425.53,"Not like us."],[2426.65,"They're not interested in preserving their traditions."],[2428.73,"And they're the same as their founding."],[2432.17,"So Genesis doesn't make sense as a post-exilic document."],[2437.37,"It just doesn't."],[2439.75,"As much as, you know, picking apart verse by verse and ascribing them to different sources is fun if you're German. on a hot afternoon"],[2448.67,"Um the dog don't hunt."],[2452.39,"Second thing I'm going to point out."],[2454.95,"And it gets obscured by how it's translated here in the Orthodox Study Bible."],[2460.23,"Which there's no real excuse for."],[2479.55,"That's how it's translated."],[2482.19,"What if I translated it the same way it's translated in the New Testament and said to you, his seed shall become the fullness of the Gentiles?"],[2491.07,"Heard that somewhere before."],[2494.13,"It's the same Greek."],[2495.49,"This is translated from the Greek."],[2496.77,"That's why I say there's no excuse."],[2498.37,"They're translated from the Hebrew."],[2499.73,"There might be an excuse for not making the connection with what St."],[2502.37,"Paul says in Romans 11."],[2505.49,"Okay? So there is this prophecy here about what Ephraim's destiny in particular is."],[2517.81,"And here it's just said in this very vague sort of way that attaches Ephraim's destiny to a particular part of the promises to Abraham."],[2533.49,"Which is the all of the nations will be blessed in you, all the nations of the world, the 70 nations for the table of nations here in Genesis."],[2542.07,"Will be blessed through you."],[2547.11,"And the way this is going to be understood by a whole lot of people, including St."],[2552.63,"Paul, but people leading up to St."],[2554.55,"Paul, including in the Testament of the 12th Patriarchs that I mentioned earlier in the Testament of Naphtali, is that the reason for the destruction of the northern kingdom, the reason for the existence of the northern kingdom and these tribes."],[2572.33,"And they're being part of Israel at all, and then them being wiped out."],[2577.33,"They're not wiped out as in in the Assyrians come and literally murder every single one of them."],[2578.53,"the Assyrians come in and They're wiped out because, what do they do?"],[2587.69,"They deport the people, and they bring people from another part of their empire and put them there."],[2595.83,"This is why the Samaritans were so hated, and they're like, you're not real Israelites."],[2601.95,"And they took the people of the Northern Kingdom and put them in other places, other Assyrian cities."],[2609.55,"And uh an old professor of mine actually has spent the last many years reading cuneiform tablets, Assyrian tablets, and tracking down where all those tribes ended up."],[2622.27,"We actually know, they're not lost."],[2623.95,"But he went looking for names"],[2625.01,"He's got lectures about it online."],[2629.01,"He went looking for Israelite names on like trade documents and sales receipts, super exciting tablets to read."],[2637.91,"But he looked for those names."],[2638.95,"And you can he identified the cities where they took all of those deportees, and after about three generations, you don't see any more Israelite names."],[2650.23,"Because they just married into and were culturally assimilated, by the nations."],[2657.73,"What you start seeing in the post-exilic period because there are all of these promises in the prophets that we'll get to in a few years, that not only is Judah going to be restored, but that all of Israel is going to be restored."],[2675.27,"That Ephraim in particular is going to be restored."],[2678.87,"Well, how is that going to happen?"],[2681.27,"They're going to come back to this, and they're going to say the northern kingdom of Israel, those ten tribes got scattered like seed out into the nations."],[2694.47,"And so there's going to be this harvest where people from all these nations come back."],[2705.71,"Come back and reconstitute those ten tribes"],[2710.65,"From among the Gentiles"],[2713.81,"And like I said, in some of the Second Temple Literature, like the Testament of Naphtali from the third century BC, it just straight out says, through Joseph, the Assyrians, the Babylonians, names a whole bunch of Gentiles, will receive a portion of the inheritance of Abraham."],[2728.53,"Like it's not subtle."],[2732.37,"And when you know that's out there, and you read St."],[2734.61,"Paul in Romans 11, and he says, Judea has been hardened."],[2741.49,"What he literally says is Judah has been hardened until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and then all Israel will be saved."],[2753.51,"So he cites this."],[2759.33,"So, on the one hand, the preservation of it and how it's preserved shows us the antiquity of the text."],[2765.19,"On the other hand, that's how we should understand the prophecy here concerning Ephraim."],[2770.59,"It's not that, oh, Ephraim was supposed to be this, and somehow the promise failed or fizzled or whatever."],[2777.63,"It's that Ephraim has this special destiny."],[2783.09,"That's going to be part and parcel of that part of the promises to Abraham being fulfilled."],[2791.41,"And Judah. Because Reuben and Sibion got tossed, Levi. and Judah, who are three and four, end up with this other special role"],[2805.11,"Or special roles in terms of the priesthood and the monarchy that emerge."],[2813.43,"But Ephraim is here, and thereby the northern kingdom and the rest of those tribes is being given this other special role."],[2819.51,"Both have a part to play."],[2832.55,"You notice you don't see Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph, or Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and Judah."],[2839.19,"It's Abraham, Isaac, Jacob."],[2842.23,"And then it gets a little more diffuse."],[2845.29,"Where these different tribes, these different peoples have different roles to play in terms of the fulfillment of the whole."],[2859.45,"Verse 20, so he blessed them that day, saying, By you Israel will be blessed, saying, May God make you as Ephraim and as Manasseh."],[2869.93,"Thus he set Ephraim before Manasseh."],[2877.13,"Then Israel said to Joseph, Behold, I'm about to die, but God will be with you and bring you back to the land of your fathers."],[2884.97,"Moreover, I have given you to you one portion above your brothers."],[2888.85,"I am giving you Shechem, which I took from the hand of the Amorites with my sword and bow."],[2895.43,"So he says, I'm giving you this one area in particular."],[2901.03,"Isn't that the place that God massacred him?"],[2903.59,"Yes. Yes. Shame on you, Reuben."],[2908.15,"A little bit of revisionist history here."],[2910.71,"Well, isn't one of the sons the sword and the other the bow?"],[2913.67,"Yeah, I mean we could say that, yeah."],[2918.63,"I mean, Reuben did something else bad too, remember."],[2922.39,"Sleeping with. Yeah."],[2925.03,"His father's wives."],[2929.19,"Listen next time, as Father Stephen DeYoung continues. his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2936.51,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."],[2941.87,"That's wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.04,"lv":2,"h":"Where we are: end of Genesis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>Genesis 48:1</b>; \"may well finish Genesis this evening\" - predictions \"usually wrong\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chs 48-49</b> = their own textual unit</li><li class=\"d0\">Last time: finished <b>Joseph story</b> = extension of <b>Jacob's story</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob traveled to <b>Egypt</b>, reunited with Joseph</li><li class=\"d1\">brothers, whole household now in <b>Nile Delta</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis = <b>prologue to the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Exodus-Deuteronomy = arc of <b>Moses' life</b> (Exodus begins with birth, Deuteronomy ends with death)</li><li class=\"d1\">Exodus opens <b>centuries later</b> - very different Pharaoh/Israel relationship</li></ul>"},{"t":105.2,"lv":3,"h":"Famine and the shift in Egypt","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Transformation in Egyptian life begun: <b>7-year famine</b> crisis</li><li class=\"d1\">Joseph prepared; measures with Pharaoh → <b>centralization of authority</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>economic shifts</b> - play into Exodus</li><li class=\"d0\">Roots of the later change are in this crisis</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Historical verisimilitude</b>: major changes in national order come in <b>times of crisis</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"just a truth throughout history\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fruit of it seen when we start <b>Exodus</b></li></ul>"},{"t":227.92,"lv":2,"h":"The Testament of Jacob","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chs 48-49 = <b>testament of Jacob</b> - as in \"last will and testament\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Parting words</b> to sons, grandsons, extended family before death</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophetic</b> in the common sense = predicting the future</li><li class=\"d1\">says something about the <b>character of descendants</b> - tribes named after them</li><li class=\"d1\">another way Genesis is <b>prologue</b> to rest of Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep in mind: about <b>later people groups</b>, not the individuals</li><li class=\"d1\">some sound harsh: \"was Jacob on his deathbed just really ticked off at one of his kids?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"imagine if those were your dad's last words to you\" - directed at the <b>tribe</b> that will bear the name</li><li class=\"d0\">Fuller sense of \"prophetic\" later, with <b>Moses</b> as prophet</li></ul>"},{"t":340.09,"lv":3,"h":"Get genetics out of your head","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Suspend the idea of <b>genetics</b> - didn't exist when this was written</li><li class=\"d0\">Exodus makes plain: tribe of <b>Dan</b> mostly not biological descendants of Dan</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Levites</b> mostly not descended from the person Levi</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not revisionist\" - read the text closely</li><li class=\"d0\">Name + \"father\" = <b>founding father</b> sense, like the US</li><li class=\"d1\">most who call <b>George Washington</b> father of the country aren't his descendants</li><li class=\"d1\">\"fatherhood is doing different work than just biological work\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>phenotype</b>: most in ancient Near East looked \"roughly identical\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no pale blue-eyed blondes; not <b>race</b> as we think of it</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>DNA testing</b> - no way to prove descent</li></ul>"},{"t":485.62,"lv":3,"h":"Rituals make a people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">People groups = <b>cultural and societal bonds</b>, based in <b>rituals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">coming-of-age rituals, rituals at <b>birth of a child</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Super important concept\" for the rest of the Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Right now: <b>no nation Israel</b> - a <b>family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">technically <b>Mesopotamians</b>; mostly biological descendants of Abraham (counting servants)</li><li class=\"d0\">Rest of Torah = <b>defining what \"Israel\" means</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>circumcision</b> / the covenant ritual</li><li class=\"d1\">coming-of-age rituals, ritual <b>worship patterns</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worship directed to a <b>particular God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>feasts and festivals</b> celebrated together</li><li class=\"d0\">These <b>identify a people group</b></li></ul>"},{"t":560.52,"lv":3,"h":"Adoption into a people: Caleb","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Can be <b>adopted into</b> a people group → just as much a member as one born in</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Caleb</b> (of Joshua and Caleb)</li><li class=\"d1\">introduced as a <b>Kenizzite</b> - not Israelite, literally a <b>Canaanite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">end of <b>Joshua</b>: one of the <b>elders of the tribe of Judah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he and descendants: \"we're <b>Judahites</b>, Judah is our father\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not true from a biological perspective\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: shift in the <b>New Testament</b>? (\"children of Abraham... raise them up from stones\")</li><li class=\"d1\">shift happens in <b>latter Old Testament</b> - <b>Ezra and Nehemiah</b>, after exile</li></ul>"},{"t":645.31,"lv":3,"h":"Intermarriage and the exile","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prohibitions on <b>intermarriage</b> come <b>after the exile</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Joseph marries Aseneth</b>, an <b>Egyptian</b> - no issue</li><li class=\"d1\">only told not to marry certain <b>Canaanite groups</b>; no general prohibition</li><li class=\"d0\">Then you can speak of an <b>ethnic Judean</b> - \"even then, not impermeable\"</li><li class=\"d0\">After exile: people in the land who allied with returnees told \"you're now members of <b>Issachar</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">genetically not - but now Israelites, so <b>can intermarry</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Maccabees</b>: treaty of <b>Hasmoneans</b> and <b>Sparta</b> - Spartans \"fellow descendants of Abraham\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like, what?\" - more on this at start of <b>Deuteronomy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":748.26,"lv":3,"h":"Salvation was never \"ethnic then universal\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Drummed in from \"a frankly bad reading of the New Testament\":</li><li class=\"d1\">OT = salvation/people of God <b>ethnic</b>; NT = <b>open to everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"simply not true\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ancient Israel</b> early on \"just as ethnically diverse as the church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">varies at different points in OT history</li><li class=\"d0\">Coming: \"crazy stuff\" - <b>third high priest</b> of Israel a <b>black African</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"put that in a movie today, everyone would say woke\" - actually <b>historically accurate</b></li></ul>"},{"t":807.81,"lv":2,"h":"Twelve tribes and the testament genre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jacob speaking to Dan, Judah, Joseph = really speaking of <b>those peoples</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Twelve tribes introduced</b> via these prophetic words, ready for <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Testament</b> format = staple of <b>Jewish literature</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tons, mostly <b>4th c. BC to 1st c. AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Testament of Adam</b> - last words to Seth and Cain (negative to Cain, positive to Seth)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Testament of Moses</b>, of <b>Enoch</b>, of <b>Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Testament of the Twelve Patriarchs</b> - each of Jacob's 12 sons' parting words</li><li class=\"d0\">Twelve Patriarchs = important <b>apocryphal text</b></li><li class=\"d1\">copies from <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b> and from <b>Mount Athos</b> (copied 15 centuries by monks)</li><li class=\"d0\">Format = vehicle to speak <b>prophetically</b>: recount Israel's history, cast own generation a certain way</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Originates with these chapters</b> → important for Torah interpretation in OT period</li><li class=\"d0\">No evidence of same weight in <b>rabbinic Judaism</b> - seen as just tied to rest of Hebrew Bible</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>critical for writing the New Testament</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1021.97,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 48:1 - Joseph brings his sons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:1</b> Joseph told father is sick → takes <b>Manasseh and Ephraim</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Manasseh</b> = older</li><li class=\"d0\">Joseph goes <b>before his brothers</b> to get the blessing</li><li class=\"d1\">tied to being <b>firstborn of Rachel</b>, favored by Jacob</li></ul>"},{"t":1071.43,"lv":3,"h":"The half-tribes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Manasseh and Ephraim sometimes called <b>half tribes</b> in English</li><li class=\"d1\">both descended from <b>Joseph</b>; no tribe \"Joseph\"</li><li class=\"d1\">other tribes named for Joseph's brothers</li><li class=\"d0\">Technically <b>13 tribes</b> though we say 12</li><li class=\"d1\">at the Ten Commandments: really <b>11</b> - why everyone numbers differently</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b> list: <b>Dan left out</b>, Manasseh and Ephraim listed separately</li><li class=\"d1\">add Dan in → 13</li><li class=\"d0\">Tally: <b>11 plus two halves</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1177.14,"lv":2,"h":"48:2-7 - \"My God appeared to me\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:2</b> Israel <b>strengthens himself</b>, sits up on the bed</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob: God <b>appeared</b> to me at <b>Luz</b> in Canaan - promise of increase, a <b>gathering of nations</b>, land as everlasting possession</li><li class=\"d1\">notice <b>\"appeared to me\"</b> - hasn't happened with Joseph; the <b>transition</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:5</b> Ephraim and Manasseh are <b>mine, like Reuben and Simeon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: isn't that Reuben and Simeon... of Shechem? - yes, <b>led the massacre</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more <b>favoritism</b>: treats Joseph's sons as his own → <b>shares of inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Who gets the <b>promises from Abraham/Isaac</b> - Jacob or Esau? Jacob now gives to <b>Joseph's line</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Joseph \"not his firstborn by a long shot\" - done <b>intentionally</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Telegraphed subtly</b>: \"Ephraim and Manasseh\" - <b>reverse order</b>; unsubtle in a minute</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:6</b> later offspring inherit <b>within</b> Manasseh/Ephraim</li><li class=\"d1\">that's why <b>two half tribes</b>, not three thirds</li></ul>"},{"t":1345.82,"lv":3,"h":"48:7 - Rachel buried at Ephrath","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rachel died beside me on the way; buried at <b>Ephrath</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"That is Bethlehem\"</b> - very clearly <b>added later</b> to say where Ephrath is</li><li class=\"d0\">Bethlehem still called <b>Ephrathah</b> later (just past <b>Christmas</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First appearance</b> of the city</li><li class=\"d0\">Important when <b>David</b> born there - almost a <b>millennium later</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1405.31,"lv":2,"h":"48:8-14 - Blessing with crossed hands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:8</b> \"Who are these?\" - not literal; asking for presentation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eyes dim</b> with age - recalls <b>Isaac blind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clearly intends to pass <b>promises and inheritance</b> on</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:11</b> \"I had not thought to see your face\" - thought Joseph dead</li><li class=\"d0\">Normal: <b>firstborn at right hand</b>, second at left - inheritance through firstborn line</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:14</b> <b>crosses arms</b>, right hand on <b>Ephraim</b>, the younger</li><li class=\"d1\">firstborn blessing to the younger - <b>just like Jacob/Esau</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not because he's blind, not confused\" - <b>on purpose</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ephraim receives the blessing of the fathers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: related to <b>crossing</b> in worship/liturgy? - no, \"I'll think more about that\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1578.52,"lv":2,"h":"48:15-16 - The Angel who redeemed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:15-16</b> \"God before whom my fathers walked... the God who has fed me... the <b>angel</b> who redeemed me\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Three in parallel</b>: God of Abraham and Isaac / God who fed me / the <b>Angel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OSB translation \"great\": <b>\"these lads\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Name</b> named upon them - and fathers' names; multitude in the earth</li></ul>"},{"t":1616.8,"lv":3,"h":"A second figure, differentiated yet equated with God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The <b>Angel</b> = the one he <b>wrestled</b> with, at the <b>ladder</b>, the <b>stone</b>, who appeared to him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sets us up for rest of Torah</b>: a <b>second figure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one of the first times called <b>Angel / Messenger</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>differentiated from God and equated with God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some <b>church fathers</b>: the angel on the ladder = <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jacob understands</b>: has not seen God, <b>and</b> has seen God - same God</li><li class=\"d1\">all \"within his understanding\" - like <b>inspiration</b> (not conscious of writing revelation)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Trinity / divinity of Christ</b> not a 4th-c. invention read back in</li><li class=\"d1\">exists in <b>Judaism</b> 5 centuries before Christ - \"here in the Torah\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mosaic origin</b>: Moses' own understanding at the root</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they do not think there's only one God, one person, no one has ever seen\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they record <b>eating with him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1861.91,"lv":2,"h":"48:17-19 - Ephraim over Manasseh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:17</b> Joseph displeased, tries to <b>move hand</b></li><li class=\"d1\">irony: Joseph, the <b>eleventh-born</b>, insists on firstborn</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:19</b> \"I know, my son, I know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Manasseh also a people, exalted - but <b>younger brother greater</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Ephraim's seed <b>a multitude of nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Important thing just happened, not obvious in the translation</li></ul>"},{"t":1919.01,"lv":3,"h":"Zera: the singular and plural promise","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Promises to Abraham's <b>seed</b> - singular <b>and</b> plural</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <b>zera</b> = collective noun, like <b>fish or sheep</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>deliberate ambiguity</b> in Genesis: it's both</li><li class=\"d0\">Plural: <b>Abraham's descendants</b>; singular: <b>the line</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaac not Ishmael</b> (Ishmael gets inheritance too)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jacob not Esau</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Joseph</b> among brothers</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ephraim not Manasseh</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: anyone who knows the OT see a problem? - not how it works out</li></ul>"},{"t":2024.64,"lv":3,"h":"Ephraim and a short timeline","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ephraim = \"head of the world\": <b>most numerous tribe</b>, center of <b>Northern Kingdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as opposed to <b>Judah</b> at the split</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep timetable in mind - \"inflated\" sense of how long Israel existed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>United Kingdom</b> < a century (<b>10th c. BC</b>) - \"eye blink\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Northern Kingdom</b> < 200 years, then gone</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Judah</b> barely 300</li><li class=\"d1\">before <b>1948</b>, nation of Israel < <b>300 years</b> in all human history</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Nations are not around very long\"</li><li class=\"d1\">any European country longer; <b>France</b>, <b>England</b>; US rivaling Northern Kingdom already</li></ul>"},{"t":2143.0,"lv":3,"h":"Antiquity of the Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First point: attestation to <b>antiquity of the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>19th-c. German critics</b>: some bits from 10th c. BC; now \"all <b>post-exile</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Ephraimite traditions if written after <b>Ephraim ceased to exist</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Samaria</b> now home of <b>Samaritans</b>, hated and despised</li><li class=\"d1\">you'd get a clear <b>pro-Judah</b> message</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Joshua, Judges</b> very pro-Judah, pro-Judahite monarchy → written post-David</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah keeps traditions of <b>tribes that ceased to exist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">main body must predate <b>dividing of the kingdom</b> (with later additions like \"which is Bethlehem\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: must be <b>older</b> to carry weight of copying</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>altars</b> in territories of the 12 tribes: traditions from different tribes brought together</li><li class=\"d0\">North and South <b>hated each other</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Syro-Ephraimite War</b>: Ephraim + Syria attack Judah</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Athaliah</b>, Ahab's daughter, tries to exterminate <b>line of David</b>; Judahites exterminate <b>line of Ahab</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody in Judah wants to preserve traditions of dead tribes - \"they're dead because they were wicked\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis doesn't make sense as post-exilic</b> - \"the dog don't hunt\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2452.39,"lv":3,"h":"\"Fullness of the Gentiles\" - Ephraim's destiny","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second point: obscured in the <b>OSB</b> translation - \"no real excuse\"</li><li class=\"d1\">translated from <b>Greek</b>; same Greek as NT: <b>\"fullness of the Gentiles\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">connection to <b>Romans 11</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ephraim's destiny tied to <b>all nations blessed in you</b> (the <b>70 nations</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Understood by many incl. <b>Paul</b> and <b>Testament of Naphtali</b>: reason for Northern Kingdom's fate</li><li class=\"d1\">not literally murdered: <b>Assyrians deport</b> them, import others</li><li class=\"d1\">why <b>Samaritans</b> hated: \"not real Israelites\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Old professor tracked <b>cuneiform tablets</b> - Israelite names on trade documents and receipts</li><li class=\"d1\">tribes <b>not lost</b>; after ~<b>3 generations</b> no more Israelite names - <b>assimilated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Post-exile: prophets promise <b>all Israel</b> restored, <b>Ephraim</b> especially</li><li class=\"d1\">scattered <b>like seed</b> → <b>harvest</b> from nations reconstitutes the 10 tribes <b>from among the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Testament of Naphtali</b> (3rd c. BC): through Joseph, Assyrians, Babylonians, Gentiles get a portion of <b>Abraham's inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 11</b>: <b>Judah hardened until fullness of the Gentiles comes in</b>, then <b>all Israel saved</b> - cites this</li><li class=\"d0\">Not that the promise \"fizzled\": <b>Ephraim has special destiny</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Levi and Judah</b> (3rd and 4th) get priesthood and monarchy after Reuben/Simeon tossed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Abraham, Isaac, Jacob\" - <b>not</b> \"and Joseph/Judah\" - diffuse roles for the whole</li></ul>"},{"t":2859.45,"lv":2,"h":"48:20-22 - Blessing sealed, Shechem given","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:20</b> \"May God make you as <b>Ephraim and Manasseh</b>\" - set <b>Ephraim before Manasseh</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:21</b> \"I'm about to die, but God will be with you\" - <b>bring you back</b> to land of fathers</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>48:22</b> one portion above your brothers: <b>Shechem</b>, taken with <b>sword and bow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: isn't that where <b>Simeon and Levi massacred</b> them? - yes; \"a little revisionist history\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: sword and bow = the sons? - \"we could say that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Reuben did something else bad too: sleeping with <b>his father's wife</b></li></ul>"}]});
